var tafsirData_18 = {
  "1": "All gratitude, appreciation, and glory belong exclusively to Allah. This opening establishes that everything good, especially the gift of divine guidance, deserves heartfelt thanks to the Creator. When someone gives you a precious gift, you thank them—how much more should we thank Allah for the greatest gift: His guidance through the Quran.\n\nAllah sent down the Quran to Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), who is described here as 'His servant.' This is actually the highest title of honor—being a true servant of Allah. The Prophet received this revelation not because of worldly status, but because of his complete devotion to Allah. The 'Book' refers to the Quran, the final scripture for all of humanity.\n\nThe Quran contains absolutely no errors, contradictions, or deviations from the truth. Unlike human writings that may have mistakes, biases, or inconsistencies, Allah's Book is perfectly straight and flawless. This addresses any doubts people might have about scripture—the Quran stands as a perfect, uncorrupted message from the Creator.",
  "2": "The Quran is completely balanced and straight, guiding people to what is right without any deviation. It serves as the standard of truth against which everything else can be measured. Just as a perfectly straight ruler helps you draw accurate lines, the Quran helps you navigate life correctly.\n\nOne purpose of the Quran is to warn those who reject faith about the serious punishment that awaits them from Allah. This warning comes from a place of mercy—like warning someone about a danger ahead on the road. Allah doesn't want anyone to face this punishment, which is why He sent the warning in advance.\n\nThe Quran also brings wonderful news to those who believe and act righteously. Notice that true faith is connected to good deeds—they go hand in hand. The 'fine reward' refers to Paradise and Allah's pleasure. This balance of warning and good news shows that the Quran addresses all of humanity with both mercy and justice.",
  "3": "The reward for believers is not temporary—it is eternal. Unlike worldly pleasures that fade away, the joys of Paradise never end. This eternal nature of the reward reflects Allah's infinite generosity. The Prophet (peace be upon him) described Paradise as containing what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has ever imagined.",
  "4": "This verse specifically addresses those who claim that Allah has children—whether they say He has sons, daughters, or any offspring. Various groups throughout history have made such claims, including some who said angels were Allah's daughters, or those who attributed a son to God. This is a fundamental error about Allah's nature. Allah is the Creator, completely unique and self-sufficient. He does not have children because He has no need for them, and nothing resembles Him. The concept of offspring implies dependency and similarity to creation, which contradicts Allah's absolute uniqueness.",
  "5": "Those who make this claim have no proof or true knowledge to support it. They simply inherited this false belief from their ancestors, who also had no evidence. This teaches us an important lesson: blindly following traditions without verification can lead to serious errors. Just because something has been believed for generations doesn't make it true.\n\nAllah expresses how grave and monstrous this statement is. Attributing offspring to the Creator is not a small matter—it is a tremendous falsehood that diminishes Allah's majesty and perfection. The Arabic emphasizes how enormous and ugly this claim is.\n\nTheir statement is pure fabrication with no basis in reality. It is not a misunderstanding or a slight error—it is an outright lie about the Lord of the universe. This strong language shows how seriously Allah views false claims about His nature.",
  "6": "Allah is addressing the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), who was so deeply concerned about people's disbelief that it caused him immense grief. The Prophet loved humanity and desperately wanted everyone to be saved from punishment. His compassion was so intense that Allah had to comfort him and tell him not to destroy himself with sorrow. This shows the Prophet's incredible mercy toward all people.\n\nThe Prophet's grief was specifically over those who rejected the Quran's message. However, Allah is reminding him that guidance is ultimately in Allah's hands. A caller to truth can only deliver the message—they cannot force hearts to accept. This is a lesson for all who invite others to good: do your best, but don't let rejection break you, because results belong to Allah.",
  "7": "Allah beautified the earth with all its attractions—wealth, status, nature, relationships, pleasures—as decorations. These are not bad in themselves; they are blessings from Allah. However, understanding their purpose is crucial. They are meant to be tools, not goals. Like decorations in a testing hall, they are part of the examination environment.\n\nThe real purpose of life's attractions is to test people. The test is not about who accumulates the most, but who uses these blessings in the best way. 'Best in deeds' means actions that are sincere (done for Allah alone) and correct (according to the Prophet's guidance). A person might have little wealth but use it excellently, while another might have much but fail the test. The quality of deeds matters more than quantity.",
  "8": "Eventually, all the beauty and attractions of this world will be destroyed, leaving the earth completely barren like a dry, lifeless desert. This reminds us that worldly things are temporary. No matter how beautiful or valuable something seems now, it will all perish. This perspective helps believers not become overly attached to worldly possessions and keeps focus on what truly lasts: good deeds and the afterlife.",
  "9": "Allah is asking the Prophet (and by extension, all of us) whether he considered the story of these young men in the cave to be the most amazing of Allah's signs. The 'plaque' likely refers to an inscription or tablet connected to their story. The point is that while this story is indeed miraculous, Allah's signs and wonders are countless. The creation of the heavens, the resurrection of the dead, and countless other signs are equally or even more wondrous. This story is one example among many of Allah's power.",
  "10": "These were young people who believed in Allah at a time when their society was deeply immersed in disbelief and idol worship. Facing persecution, they had to flee and hide in a cave. Youth is often a time of following desires and peer pressure, but these young men chose faith over comfort. Their story shows that age is no excuse for not standing for truth.\n\nTheir first request was for Allah's special mercy—not just any mercy, but mercy directly from Him. They recognized that in their difficult situation, only Allah's mercy could save and protect them. This is a beautiful supplication that shows complete dependence on Allah.\n\nTheir second request was for guidance to handle their situation correctly. They didn't just ask for the problem to disappear; they asked for wisdom to navigate through it. This teaches us that when facing trials, we should ask Allah for both His mercy and His guidance to respond appropriately.",
  "11": "Allah answered their prayer by making them fall into a deep, uninterrupted sleep for many years. The expression 'struck upon their ears' means their hearing was sealed so nothing could disturb them. This miraculous sleep was Allah's way of protecting them from the persecution of their time. While they slept, the world around them completely changed.",
  "12": "Allah then awakened them from their long sleep. The purpose was to demonstrate which group—the youths themselves or others who would later debate their story—could more accurately estimate how long they had slept. This awakening served as a sign and proof of Allah's power, particularly regarding resurrection. If Allah can make people sleep for centuries and wake them up, surely He can resurrect the dead on the Day of Judgment.",
  "13": "Allah is telling the Prophet that this account is completely accurate and true—unlike the distorted versions that may have circulated among people. When Allah narrates a story, it is the definitive, correct version. This assures us that we can trust every detail mentioned in the Quran.\n\nAllah confirms they were young people with sincere faith. Their youth is significant because young people often face stronger temptations and peer pressure, yet these individuals chose faith. The Prophet (peace be upon him) mentioned that one of the seven types of people who will be shaded by Allah on the Day of Judgment is 'a youth who grew up worshipping Allah.'\n\nBecause they took the first step of believing, Allah rewarded them with even more guidance. This is a consistent principle: whoever sincerely seeks guidance, Allah increases them in it. It's like a person walking toward a light—the closer they get, the brighter it becomes. Guidance attracts more guidance.",
  "14": "Allah tied their hearts firmly to faith, giving them courage and steadfastness. Without this divine support, standing against an entire society's false beliefs would have been overwhelming. This shows that true courage comes from Allah—we should always ask Him to strengthen our hearts when facing difficulties.\n\nThey publicly proclaimed their faith, making a clear theological statement: the true Lord is the Creator of everything in existence. Standing up here means they took a public stance for truth, which required tremendous bravery in a hostile environment. They connected their personal Lord ('Our Lord') to the universal Lord of all creation.\n\nThey made a firm commitment to pure monotheism—worshipping Allah alone without any partners. This wasn't just a belief they held privately; it was a declaration they were willing to defend. They drew a clear line that they would never cross.\n\nThey recognized that associating partners with Allah would be a monstrous statement—far from truth and reason. They understood that claiming other gods exist alongside Allah is not a minor error but a tremendous falsehood against the Creator of the universe.",
  "15": "The youths discussed among themselves how their own community had adopted false gods. Despite these being their own people—their families, neighbors, and society—they could clearly see the error. This shows that truth must be recognized even when it means acknowledging that those closest to us are wrong.\n\nThey challenged the logical basis of idol worship: where is the evidence? If these gods were real and worthy of worship, there should be clear proof. This teaches critical thinking in matters of faith—true belief should be based on evidence and reason, not blind tradition. The idols their people worshipped had no power, gave no revelation, and showed no signs of divinity.\n\nThey concluded that inventing false gods and attributing partners to Allah is the greatest form of wrongdoing and injustice. It is a lie against the Creator Himself. This rhetorical question emphasizes that there is no greater crime than creating falsehood about Allah.",
  "16": "Having made the decision to separate from their society's false worship, the youths had already taken the crucial first step. Sometimes preserving one's faith requires physical separation from environments of corruption and disbelief. They chose Allah over social acceptance.\n\nThey advised each other to seek shelter in the cave as a practical step for safety. When facing persecution, taking reasonable measures for protection is wise and permissible. They combined trust in Allah with practical action.\n\nThey expressed optimism and hope in Allah's mercy. Despite their difficult circumstances—being young, alone, and persecuted—they had complete confidence that Allah would take care of them. This positive expectation of Allah (husn al-dhann) is essential for every believer.\n\nThey believed Allah would make their difficult situation manageable and provide them ease and comfort. And indeed, Allah did exactly that by protecting them miraculously for centuries. When we trust Allah and take steps for His sake, He facilitates our affairs in ways we cannot imagine.",
  "17": "Allah describes how the sun's rays would move away from the cave's entrance when rising, not directly hitting the sleeping youths. This was a miraculous arrangement to protect them from the heat and exposure of direct sunlight.\n\nSimilarly, in the evening, the setting sun would pass by without its rays entering the cave directly. Throughout the day, they were shielded from the sun. This perfect positioning was not coincidence—it was Allah's deliberate protection of His believing servants.\n\nDespite being in an open area of the cave where fresh air could circulate, they remained protected from the sun. This balance ensured they had ventilation to survive but protection from harmful exposure. Every detail of their preservation was orchestrated by Allah.\n\nAllah points out that this arrangement is a clear sign of His power and care. The entire scenario—the cave's position, the sun's path, their miraculous sleep—all testify to Allah's complete control over creation.\n\nThis statement establishes that real guidance comes only from Allah. The youths were guided because Allah chose to guide them. If someone receives guidance from Allah, they are truly on the right path.\n\nConversely, if Allah does not guide someone, no one else can guide them. This emphasizes our need to constantly ask Allah for guidance, as it is entirely in His hands. Without His guidance, all efforts are futile.",
  "18": "Anyone looking at them would have assumed they were not sleeping—perhaps their eyes were partially open or their appearance suggested wakefulness. This added to the miraculous nature of their state and likely deterred anyone who might have approached.\n\nDespite appearing awake, they were in fact in a deep, miraculous sleep that lasted for centuries. This was not ordinary sleep—it was a special state that preserved their bodies without the need for food, water, or normal bodily functions.\n\nAllah caused them to turn from side to side during their sleep. This prevented their bodies from deteriorating due to lying in one position for too long—like how bedsores develop in patients who don't move. Allah preserved their physical well-being through this miraculous turning.\n\nTheir dog lay at the cave's entrance with its front legs extended, as if guarding them. This loyal animal was blessed by being in the company of righteous people. Scholars note that even an animal benefits from association with the pious. The dog served as an additional deterrent to anyone approaching.\n\nAllah placed such awe and fear upon their appearance that anyone who saw them would run away terrified. This was another layer of divine protection—even if someone found the cave, they would not dare approach or harm the sleepers. Allah protects His servants through both visible and invisible means.",
  "19": "After their long sleep, Allah woke them up. The purpose was for them to discuss among themselves and realize the miracle that had occurred. Their conversation would reveal just how extraordinary their situation was.\n\nUpon waking, one of them naturally asked about the duration of their sleep. This is a normal human reaction after waking from what seemed like a long rest. Little did they know the true answer would be beyond anything they could imagine.\n\nTheir estimate was completely off—they had slept for centuries but felt like it was just a day or less. This shows how subjective our perception of time can be. It also illustrates how death and resurrection will feel—people will be resurrected and feel as if they had only slept briefly.\n\nShowing wisdom and humility, they ultimately left the knowledge of the true duration to Allah. When we don't have certain knowledge, the proper response is to acknowledge Allah's knowledge rather than argue. This is a beautiful example of intellectual humility.\n\nThey decided to send someone to buy food with the old coins they had. Their request for 'the purest food' shows their continued consciousness about consuming only what is halal and wholesome. Even in their hunger, they prioritized quality and permissibility over convenience.\n\nThe practical matter of getting food was important, showing that believers deal with worldly necessities while maintaining their principles. They organized themselves sensibly despite their unusual circumstances.\n\nThey emphasized the need for extreme care and discretion. The one going to the city should be gentle, wise, and subtle in his approach to avoid drawing attention.\n\nTheir concern was that if the authorities discovered them, they would face persecution. This shows that they were still thinking clearly and acting wisely despite their miraculous experience.",
  "20": "They understood the severe consequences if caught—execution by stoning. This was the penalty their persecutors would impose on them for their beliefs. Their fear was based on real threats they had faced before their escape.\n\nThe alternative to death was forced apostasy—being compelled to return to idol worship and disbelief. For these faithful youths, this was equally unacceptable. They would rather face death than abandon their faith.\n\nThey recognized that abandoning faith for worldly safety would mean eternal failure. Success in this context means success in the afterlife. Even if one survives in this world by compromising faith, they have truly lost. This perspective prioritizes eternal success over temporary survival.",
  "21": "When one of the youths went to the city with ancient coins, people recognized that something extraordinary was happening. The outdated currency revealed that these were people from a different era. Allah orchestrated this discovery for a greater purpose.\n\nThe primary purpose of this miracle was to prove that resurrection is real. If Allah can preserve people asleep for centuries and wake them up as if no time had passed, surely He can resurrect the dead on the Day of Judgment. Their awakening was a powerful demonstration of Allah's promise.\n\nThe 'Hour' refers to the Day of Judgment. This entire miracle served as clear evidence that the Hour will certainly come. The people who witnessed or heard about these youths had a living proof of Allah's power over life, death, and resurrection.\n\nAfter the youths eventually died (presumably soon after their discovery, having fulfilled their purpose), people debated what to do to honor them. This disputes shows how people were moved by their story.\n\nOne group suggested simply building a protective structure over them as a memorial. This was a modest proposal to honor their memory.\n\nThis phrase shows humility—the people acknowledged that only Allah truly knows everything about these blessed youths. It's a reminder that we should focus on lessons rather than getting caught up in details we cannot verify.\n\nThe group that had more influence decided to build a mosque or place of worship at the site. This indicates that by the time of their discovery, society had shifted toward belief in Allah. However, scholars note that building places of worship over graves was not approved by Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), who warned against this practice. The Quran is narrating what people did, not necessarily endorsing it.",
  "22": "Allah describes how people would speculate about the number of sleepers. One opinion held that there were three youths plus their dog. This was guesswork without evidence.\n\nAnother opinion claimed five youths with the dog as sixth. Allah characterizes this as 'guessing blindly' (literally 'stoning the unseen')—making claims about unknown matters without any proof. This describes speculation presented as fact.\n\nA third opinion stated seven youths with the dog as eighth. Scholars note that Allah did not criticize this opinion the way He criticized the previous two, leading many to believe this is the correct number. However, the exact count is not what matters most.\n\nThe Prophet is instructed to acknowledge that only Allah knows the true number. This teaches us humility when dealing with matters we cannot verify. Rather than argue endlessly about details that don't affect our faith or practice, we should acknowledge Allah's complete knowledge.\n\nVery few people actually know the correct number—perhaps only those who received direct revelation about it. Most claims are speculation. This reinforces that we shouldn't pretend to know what we don't.\n\nThe Prophet is commanded not to engage in baseless debates about the sleepers. If one must discuss such matters, it should be based on certain knowledge (like Quranic revelation), not speculation. This is guidance for all Muslims: avoid arguing about matters without proper knowledge.\n\nThe Prophet should not seek information about the sleepers from those who merely speculate. This teaches us to be careful about our sources of knowledge—we should seek information from reliable, knowledgeable sources rather than those who guess or fabricate.",
  "23": "Allah commands us never to make definite statements about future actions without acknowledging His will. We cannot guarantee what we will do tomorrow because we don't control the future—we might become ill, circumstances might change, or we might not even live until then. This verse was revealed when the Prophet was asked questions and said he would answer the next day, but revelation was delayed. It teaches us that all our plans depend on Allah's permission.",
  "24": "The solution is to always add 'In shaa Allah' (if Allah wills) when speaking about future plans. This phrase acknowledges that nothing happens except by Allah's will and permission. It keeps us humble and reminds us of our dependence on Allah. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that whoever says 'In shaa Allah' has made an exception and will not be held accountable if the plan doesn't materialize.\n\nIf someone forgets to say 'In shaa Allah,' they should remember Allah when they realize their omission. Some scholars say you can still say it even if time has passed. Others interpret this as remembering Allah in general through dhikr (remembrance). Either way, forgetting is human, and Allah provides a way to correct ourselves.\n\nThis supplication asks Allah to guide us to something better and closer to correctness than our current knowledge or plans. It shows humility—acknowledging that there might be better paths or answers that Allah knows. We should always hope that Allah will guide us to improved understanding and better outcomes.",
  "25": "Allah now reveals the actual duration of their sleep: 309 years. The phrasing 'three hundred years, adding nine' has been explained in different ways. One explanation is that 300 solar years equal approximately 309 lunar years (since the lunar calendar is about 11 days shorter per year). Thus, Allah gave both measurements. This demonstrates the Quran's precision and accommodates different calendar systems. The main point is that they slept for an extraordinarily long time, preserved miraculously by Allah.",
  "26": "Even after mentioning the duration, Allah emphasizes that He alone has perfect knowledge of exactly how long they stayed. Any human calculation could have variations, but Allah's knowledge is absolute and precise.\n\nAllah alone possesses complete knowledge of everything hidden in the entire universe—past, present, and future. No one else has access to this knowledge except what Allah reveals. The 'unseen' includes everything beyond human perception: the hidden aspects of creation, future events, and spiritual realities.\n\nThis is an exclamation of wonder at Allah's perfect hearing and seeing. Nothing escapes His perception—every whisper, every hidden thought, every movement. His hearing and seeing are complete, missing nothing and requiring no assistance.\n\nCreation has no protector or helper besides Allah. Any other supposed guardian is false and powerless. All protection, provision, and guidance ultimately come from Allah alone.\n\nAllah's authority is absolute and undivided. He does not share His decision-making or sovereignty with anyone—no partner, no equal, no assistant. What He decrees happens, and no one can override His command. This concludes the story by returning to the core message of Allah's complete oneness in His attributes and authority.",
  "27": "The Prophet (peace be upon him) is commanded to continuously recite the Quran that Allah revealed to him. This instruction extends to all believers—we should regularly recite, read, and reflect upon the Quran. It is our direct connection to Allah's guidance. The Quran is described as being 'from your Lord,' emphasizing its divine origin and the personal relationship between Allah and His messenger.\n\nAllah's words in the Quran are perfectly preserved and unchangeable. No human, no force, and no passage of time can alter what Allah has revealed. This is a guarantee of the Quran's authenticity until the Day of Judgment. Unlike previous scriptures that were altered over time, Allah Himself protects the Quran from any corruption or modification.\n\nThere is no place of safety, no protector, and no shelter except with Allah. If someone tries to flee from Allah's commands or seeks protection elsewhere, they will find none. This reminds us that our only true security is in turning to Allah and following His guidance—there is simply nowhere else to go.",
  "28": "The Prophet is commanded to keep himself in the company of sincere believers who worship Allah consistently—in the morning and evening, meaning throughout the day. These are people whose goal is Allah's pleasure, not worldly gain. This verse was revealed when some wealthy Quraysh leaders asked the Prophet to remove poor companions like Bilal, Ammar, and Salman from his gatherings. Allah rejected this, teaching that a person's value is in their faith, not their social status. We should choose friends who remind us of Allah.\n\nThe Prophet is warned not to turn his attention away from these sincere believers toward wealthy or influential people, hoping to gain worldly benefits. This teaches us not to be dazzled by wealth and status to the point of neglecting or looking down upon humble, righteous people. The poor believer who prays sincerely is more valuable than the rich person who is heedless of Allah.\n\nAllah warns against following people whose hearts are neglectful of Allah. These are people so absorbed in worldly matters that they have forgotten their Creator. Their hearts have become sealed due to their own choices, and following them leads to misguidance. A person's outward status means nothing if their heart is disconnected from Allah.\n\nSuch people are slaves to their whims and desires rather than servants of Allah. Instead of following divine guidance, they follow whatever their soul craves—whether it's wealth, pleasure, or status. This is a dangerous state because desires can lead anywhere, while revelation leads to salvation.\n\nThe affairs of such people are completely ruined and wasted. Despite any worldly success they may seem to have, their ultimate state is loss because they have neglected what truly matters. Their lives lack purpose, direction, and any lasting benefit.",
  "29": "The Prophet is commanded to declare that the Quran and its message are the absolute truth from Allah. This is not opinion, speculation, or human philosophy—it is divine truth that comes from the Lord of all creation. People can accept it or reject it, but its nature as truth does not change based on their response.\n\nThis statement establishes human free will and accountability. Allah does not force anyone to believe—each person makes their own choice. However, this freedom comes with responsibility. It's not a casual statement of indifference but a serious warning: you are free to choose, but you will bear the consequences of your choice. This refutes any idea that people are forced into faith or disbelief.\n\nFor those who choose disbelief and wrongdoing, Allah has prepared Hellfire. Its walls or canopy will completely enclose them, leaving no escape route. Unlike worldly fires that one can flee from, this fire surrounds its inhabitants from every direction. The term 'wrongdoers' here refers primarily to those who wrong themselves through disbelief.\n\nIn their desperate thirst, when they call for water, they will be given something that looks like water but is actually like boiling molten metal. Instead of providing relief, it will burn their faces as they try to drink. This is the opposite of aid—it increases their torment. The description is meant to make us fear this fate and work to avoid it.\n\nAllah Himself describes how awful this drink and this place are. The word 'rest' is used ironically—there is no rest in Hell, only continuous punishment. This exclamation emphasizes the severity of the consequences of disbelief and should motivate us toward faith and good deeds.",
  "30": "After describing the fate of disbelievers, Allah now turns to the believers. Notice that faith and good deeds are mentioned together—true belief naturally leads to righteous actions. One without the other is incomplete. Faith is in the heart, and deeds are its proof and manifestation.\n\nAllah guarantees that no good deed will go unrewarded. Not a single act of goodness, no matter how small, will be lost or overlooked. Allah is the most just and the most generous—He will never waste the efforts of those who strive to do good. This promise gives believers confidence that their struggles and sacrifices are never in vain.",
  "31": "The believers will receive Gardens of Eden—Paradise that lasts forever. Unlike worldly gardens that wither and die, these gardens are eternal. The word 'Eternity' emphasizes the permanent nature of this reward, contrasting sharply with the temporary nature of worldly pleasures.\n\nRivers will flow beneath them, through their gardens and under their palaces. These are not ordinary rivers but rivers of water, milk, honey, and pure wine as described elsewhere in the Quran. The flowing rivers add beauty, freshness, and life to Paradise.\n\nThe inhabitants of Paradise will be decorated with golden bracelets. While gold jewelry for men was forbidden in this world, in Paradise both men and women will be adorned with it. This represents honor, beauty, and the lifting of worldly restrictions in a place of pure reward.\n\nThey will wear beautiful green clothes made of the finest silk—both thin, delicate silk and thick, luxurious brocade. Green is often associated with Paradise and represents lushness and beauty. These garments will be of a quality unmatched by anything in this world.\n\nThey will relax on elevated, decorated couches—a symbol of rest, comfort, and honor. After the struggles of worldly life, believers will enjoy perfect relaxation. The image of reclining represents the complete absence of worry, work, or fatigue.\n\nAllah exclaims how excellent this reward is, directly contrasting with the earlier description of Hell. While Hell is 'terrible,' Paradise is 'marvellous.' This comparison motivates us to work toward Paradise and avoid the path to Hell.",
  "32": "Allah instructs the Prophet to present a parable to help people understand an important truth about wealth, faith, and attitude. Parables make abstract concepts concrete and relatable. This story contrasts two types of people and their ultimate outcomes.\n\nOne of the two men was blessed with two gardens full of grapevines—a valuable crop that produces fruit, shade, and income. Notice that it was Allah who gave him this wealth ('We gave'), though the man would later forget this crucial fact.\n\nThe gardens were bordered by palm trees, which provided additional fruit (dates), protection from wind, and beauty. Palm trees were highly valued in Arabian society for their many uses. The layout was both productive and aesthetically pleasing.\n\nBetween the grapevines and palm trees, there were other crops growing—maximizing the productivity of the land. This man had an agricultural paradise: grapes, dates, and various other produce. His investment was diversified and abundant.",
  "33": "Both gardens were perfectly productive—every plant gave its full yield without any reduction. There were no crop failures, no pests, no disappointing harvests. This was an exceptional blessing that many farmers never experience. Yet instead of being grateful, the owner would become arrogant.\n\nTo complete this perfect agricultural setup, Allah caused a river to flow between the two gardens, providing constant irrigation without the owner needing to dig wells or carry water. Everything was made easy for him—all provisions came from Allah.",
  "34": "Beyond the gardens, this man had additional wealth and income. He was extremely prosperous by every worldly measure. Allah had given him abundance upon abundance.\n\nInstead of being humble and grateful, he boasted to his companion who was a believer but had less wealth. During their conversation, the rich man's arrogance emerged. True character often reveals itself in how we treat those with less than us.\n\nHe declared himself superior because of his money and the number of servants, workers, or children he had. He measured human worth by material possessions—a deeply flawed perspective. He looked down on his believing companion simply because of worldly status, ignoring the value of faith.",
  "35": "He walked into his garden in a state of self-delusion and sin. By being arrogant and ungrateful, he was actually harming himself—not anyone else. When we forget Allah and become proud of our blessings, we wrong our own souls because we set ourselves up for punishment.\n\nLooking at his thriving gardens, he convinced himself that they would last forever. He had become so secure in his worldly success that he couldn't imagine it ever ending. This is the deception of worldly prosperity—it makes people feel invincible when they are actually completely dependent on Allah's will.",
  "36": "His arrogance extended to denying the Day of Judgment entirely. He doubted that there would ever be accountability for his actions. This is a common pattern: when people become intoxicated with worldly success, they often lose sight of the afterlife and imagine that the present situation will continue indefinitely.\n\nEven if there were an afterlife (which he doubted), he assumed he would receive even more blessings there. His flawed logic was: 'Since Allah gave me so much in this world, He must favor me, so the afterlife would be even better.' He failed to realize that worldly blessings are a test, not necessarily a sign of Allah's pleasure. Many disbelievers are given abundant provisions as a trial.",
  "37": "The poor but believing companion responded to the rich man's arrogance. Despite having less wealth, he had something far more valuable: faith and wisdom. He didn't remain silent in the face of disbelief but spoke up with good advice.\n\nHe reminded the arrogant man of his humble origins. Every human being ultimately traces back to Adam, who was created from dust. No matter how wealthy or powerful someone becomes, their origin is the same humble earth. This should inspire humility, not arrogance.\n\nThe reminder continues: each person began as an insignificant drop of fluid. This is our immediate origin before birth. How can someone who started as something so small and weak become arrogant before the Creator?\n\nAllah then shaped and perfected the human being into a complete person with intellect, abilities, and form. The entire process of creation—from dust to sperm to full human—was done by Allah. The One who created you from nothing certainly has power over you and your possessions.",
  "38": "The believing companion declared his own faith clearly. Despite his poverty, he knew the truth: Allah is his Lord and Master. He didn't let his companion's wealth make him doubt or feel inferior in faith. His identity and confidence came from his relationship with Allah, not from worldly possessions.\n\nHe affirmed his commitment to pure monotheism—he would never worship anything or anyone alongside Allah. This was his firm stance regardless of circumstances. While the rich man trusted in his gardens, the believing companion trusted only in Allah.",
  "39": "The believer advised what the rich man should have said: 'Maa shaa Allah' (This is what Allah willed). This phrase acknowledges that all blessings come from Allah and exist only by His will. It protects against arrogance and the evil eye. When we see something beautiful or receive a blessing, we should attribute it to Allah.\n\n'Laa quwwata illa billah' (There is no power except with Allah) acknowledges our complete dependence on Allah for everything. We have no ability to achieve, maintain, or protect anything without Allah's power. This phrase, combined with 'Maa shaa Allah,' is a powerful protection and reminder of our true state.\n\nThe believing companion acknowledged the material reality—yes, he had less money and fewer children. He wasn't denying facts or pretending to be wealthy. But he understood that these worldly measures don't determine true success or Allah's favor.",
  "40": "The believer expressed hope in Allah's generosity. He hoped that Allah might give him something superior to these impressive gardens—whether in this world or the next. His hope was in Allah, not in current circumstances.\n\nHe warned that Allah could easily destroy the gardens with a disaster from the sky—lightning, hail, or some other calamity—reducing the lush gardens to slippery, barren ground where nothing grows. This warning was not out of jealousy but to make the arrogant man realize the fragility of his possessions.",
  "41": "Another possibility: the river that irrigated the gardens could simply disappear into the ground. The water that the rich man took for granted could be taken away by Allah at any moment. Without water, the gardens would quickly die.\n\nIf Allah caused the water to sink deep into the earth, no human effort could retrieve it. Despite all his wealth and workers, the man would be helpless. This illustrates how completely dependent we are on Allah for basic necessities like water—things we often take for granted.",
  "42": "Exactly as the believer had warned, destruction came to the gardens. All the fruits, crops, and produce were completely destroyed. The thriving gardens that the man thought would last forever were ruined. Allah's decree happened swiftly and completely.\n\nThe man was overcome with regret, wringing his hands—a gesture of grief and frustration. He thought about all the money, time, and effort he had invested in those gardens, now all gone. This is the reality of worldly investments when Allah is not acknowledged.\n\nThe physical structures—the trellises that supported the grapevines—had fallen. The gardens were not just damaged but completely destroyed, lying in ruins. Everything he was so proud of was now rubble.\n\nFinally, the truth became clear to him. He realized that his real crime was not just arrogance but associating partners with Allah—whether by trusting in his wealth instead of Allah, or by his attitude of self-sufficiency. His regret came too late to save his gardens, but hopefully in time to save his soul. This shows that trials can sometimes wake people up to truth.",
  "43": "All those workers and supporters he boasted about were useless against Allah's decree. When Allah decides something, no army, no amount of money, and no connections can prevent it. The 'manpower' he was so proud of couldn't save a single grape.\n\nNot only could others not help him, but he couldn't even help himself. He was completely powerless. This is the reality of human beings before Allah—we are utterly dependent on Him, whether we acknowledge it or not. The arrogant man learned this lesson the hard way.",
  "44": "In moments of crisis, the reality becomes clear: true protection, support, and guardianship belong only to Allah, the True God. All other sources of support are illusions. When calamity strikes, only Allah can help. This is true whether we recognize it in good times or only realize it in bad times.\n\nAllah gives the best rewards to those who believe in Him and do good. His rewards are greater, more lasting, and more satisfying than anything this world can offer. The gardens of Paradise that await believers are infinitely better than any worldly garden.\n\nThe final results for those who trust Allah are the best possible outcomes. While the arrogant man's outcome was destruction, those who trust Allah will have the best endings—both in this world and the next. Allah is the best to determine outcomes because He knows what is truly good for us.",
  "45": "Allah instructs the Prophet to present another parable that illustrates the true nature of life in this world. This parable helps us understand why we shouldn't become attached to worldly things.\n\nWorldly life is compared to plants that flourish after rain. When water comes, vegetation grows lush and beautiful—full of life and color. This represents the peak of worldly success: youth, health, wealth, and beauty.\n\nBut this lush vegetation doesn't last. It dries up, becomes brittle chaff, and is blown away by the wind as if it never existed. Similarly, all worldly success—no matter how impressive—will eventually fade away and be forgotten. This is the inevitable end of everything worldly.\n\nAllah has complete power over everything. He can make things flourish, and He can make them perish. He can give, and He can take away. Understanding this should make us rely on Him rather than on temporary worldly things. His power is the only constant in a world of change.",
  "46": "Allah acknowledges that money and children beautify life in this world. They are natural blessings that humans desire and enjoy. There is nothing wrong with having them—they are from Allah. However, they are described as 'adornment'—decorations that are pleasing but not essential or permanent.\n\nWhat truly matters are good deeds that last—prayers, charity, remembrance of Allah, kindness, and all righteous actions. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained that 'the everlasting good deeds' include saying 'SubhanAllah' (Glory be to Allah), 'Alhamdulillah' (Praise be to Allah), 'La ilaha illallah' (There is no god but Allah), and 'Allahu Akbar' (Allah is the Greatest). These deeds are better in Allah's sight than all the wealth and children in the world.\n\nGood deeds also give better hope for the future. While wealth can be lost and children can disappoint or pass away, good deeds are stored safely with Allah and will benefit us on the Day of Judgment. Our hope in their reward is well-founded and will never be disappointed.",
  "47": "On the Day of Judgment, the massive mountains that seem so permanent and stable will be moved and blown away like dust or wool. If something as solid as mountains will be removed, what about human accomplishments? This imagery emphasizes the magnitude of that Day and the complete transformation of the earth.\n\nThe earth will be flattened and exposed, with nothing hidden. All the graves will be opened, all the hidden things revealed. The familiar landscape will be completely transformed into a vast plain where everyone can see and be seen. There will be nowhere to hide.\n\nEvery human being who ever lived will be gathered—no one will escape. From the first human to the last, from kings to slaves, from believers to disbelievers—all will stand before Allah. No one will be forgotten or overlooked. This comprehensive gathering shows that accountability is universal.",
  "48": "All of humanity will stand in rows before Allah for judgment. This is an organized, formal presentation—like appearing before a supreme court, but infinitely more significant. There is no confusion, no chaos—just perfect divine order as each person faces their Creator.\n\nThe deniers will be confronted with reality: they have come back to Allah just as they were first created—alone, without wealth, status, or supporters. All the things they were proud of in the world are gone. They are as bare and helpless as the day they were born.\n\nThis is a rebuke to those who denied the resurrection. They used to say there would be no Day of Judgment, no accountability, no return to Allah. Now they stand before Him, and their denial is exposed as the lie it always was. The appointment they denied has arrived.",
  "49": "The books containing everyone's deeds will be presented. Every person has angels recording their actions, and these records will be opened on that Day. Nothing was missed—every deed, word, and intention was documented.\n\nThe criminals and sinners will be terrified when they see their records. They will remember things they had forgotten, see deeds they thought were hidden, and realize the full extent of their wrongdoing. Their fear is justified because they know what they did.\n\nIn their panic, they will exclaim how comprehensive the record is. Even the smallest sins they dismissed as insignificant are there. Every major and minor wrong is listed. Nothing escaped documentation. This should make us careful about 'small' sins—they are all recorded.\n\nAll their deeds—good and bad—will be right there in front of them. There is no denying, no escaping, no hiding. The evidence is complete and undeniable. This is the ultimate moment of truth.\n\nDespite the terror of that Day, Allah's justice is perfect. No one will be punished for something they didn't do, and no one will be denied reward for good they did. Allah does not wrong anyone even by an atom's weight. If someone is punished, it is because they earned it through their own choices. This guarantee of justice should comfort the righteous and warn the wrongdoers.",
  "50": "Allah reminds us of the historic moment when He commanded the angels to bow down before Adam out of respect and honor—not worship. This prostration was a recognition of Adam's special status as Allah's vicegerent on earth and a being to whom Allah taught the names of all things.\n\nAll the angels obeyed Allah's command immediately and completely. However, Iblis (Satan) refused. He was present among the angels because of his elevated status due to his extensive worship, but when the test came, his true nature was exposed.\n\nThis verse clarifies that Iblis was not an angel but a jinn. Jinn, unlike angels, have free will and can choose to obey or disobey. Iblis chose disobedience. Despite all his previous worship, his arrogance led him to rebel against a direct command from Allah. His sin was not a mistake but a deliberate, prideful refusal. He thought he was better than Adam because he was created from fire while Adam was created from clay.\n\nAllah poses a powerful rhetorical question to humanity: After knowing that Iblis deliberately disobeyed Allah and was expelled, why would you follow him instead of your Creator? Iblis has descendants and helpers among the jinn who work with him to mislead humanity. Choosing to follow Satan's whispers over Allah's guidance is utterly irrational.\n\nAllah makes it absolutely clear: Satan and his followers are our enemies. This is not a hidden fact—Allah has told us plainly. Satan's mission is to lead humans to Hell with him. He has no love or concern for us; his goal is our destruction. Anyone who follows him is following their sworn enemy.\n\nThose who choose Satan over Allah have made a terrible exchange. They traded the guidance of the Most Merciful Creator for the deception of a jealous, vengeful enemy. This is truly the worst possible bargain anyone could make.",
  "51": "Allah clarifies that Satan and his followers were not present when Allah created the universe, nor did they witness or participate in creation in any way. They have no special knowledge about the origins of existence. So why would anyone turn to them for guidance about life's purpose when they know nothing about creation? Only the Creator knows the purpose of creation.\n\nAllah declares that He would never take those who mislead others as assistants or supporters. Satan and his followers have no role in Allah's plan except as a test. Allah's helpers are the righteous—the prophets, angels, and believers. Those who lead others astray have no authority from Allah whatsoever.",
  "52": "On the Day of Judgment, Allah will challenge those who worshipped others besides Him to call upon their false gods. This includes idols, Satan, ancestors, or anything else they devoted themselves to instead of Allah. It will be a moment of exposure and humiliation.\n\nWhen they desperately call out to their false objects of worship, there will be complete silence. No help will come. The idols cannot respond because they never had any power. Satan and his followers will abandon those they misled. Everyone will be concerned only with themselves on that Day.\n\nAllah will place a barrier or shared destruction between the misleaders and those who were misled. Both groups will share in punishment. Following someone into sin does not reduce your accountability—both the leader in evil and the follower will face consequences together.",
  "53": "The criminals and sinners will actually see Hellfire before them. At that moment, they will know with certainty that they are going to enter it. There will be no doubt, no hope of escape—just the terrifying realization of their fate. What they denied in the worldly life will be undeniably in front of them.\n\nThey will desperately look for any way to escape or avoid the Fire, but there will be none. Every exit will be blocked. In this world, there are usually alternatives or ways out of problems, but on that Day, the punishment will be inescapable for those who deserve it. This should motivate us to seek forgiveness now, while we still can.",
  "54": "Allah has presented every type of example, parable, argument, and lesson that people need for guidance in the Quran. Stories of past nations, descriptions of Paradise and Hell, logical proofs of Allah's existence, moral teachings—everything is covered. The Quran is comprehensive in its guidance.\n\nDespite all this clear guidance, humans tend to argue and dispute more than any other creation. Instead of accepting the truth, people often debate, raise objections, and look for excuses to reject. This is a sobering observation about human nature. While the ability to reason is a gift, using it to argue against clear truth is a misuse of that gift.",
  "55": "When clear guidance arrives, the logical response would be to believe and seek forgiveness. The path is made clear, the door of repentance is open—what more could one need? Yet many still refuse.\n\nSome people essentially demand to see the same punishment that destroyed previous disbelieving nations before they will believe. This is both foolish and arrogant—they want proof of punishment, not realizing that once it comes, it will be too late to benefit from belief.\n\nOthers demand to see the punishment directly in front of them before they will believe. But the whole point of faith is believing before seeing. When the punishment is right before their eyes, belief will no longer save them. The test of faith requires believing in the unseen based on clear guidance.",
  "56": "Allah clarifies the role of messengers: they bring good news of reward for believers and warnings of punishment for disbelievers. They are not sent to force anyone, perform miracles on demand, or control people's hearts. Their job is to deliver the message clearly, and they fulfilled that role.\n\nInstead of accepting the message, disbelievers use false arguments to try to defeat the truth. They raise baseless objections, create confusion, and use rhetorical tricks—all to avoid accepting what they know deep down is true. Their goal is to undermine the truth, not to sincerely seek it.\n\nBeyond arguing, they mock Allah's signs and the warnings of punishment. They treat the Quran as a joke, ridicule the concept of Judgment Day, and laugh at believers. This mockery is a serious offense because it shows contempt for Allah's message and His messengers.",
  "57": "Allah asks rhetorically: who is more unjust than the person who hears Allah's verses—clear reminders of truth—and deliberately turns away? They have been given the message, understood it, and consciously rejected it. This is the worst form of wrongdoing.\n\nThese same people ignore and forget their own sins and mistakes. They neither reflect on their past wrongs nor feel any need to repent. This combination of rejecting guidance and ignoring personal accountability is spiritually fatal.\n\nAs a consequence of their persistent rejection, Allah places coverings on their hearts so they cannot understand the Quran, and heaviness in their ears so they cannot truly hear it. This is not arbitrary—it is the result of their own choices. When someone repeatedly rejects truth, their ability to perceive it decreases.\n\nThe Prophet is told that no matter how much he calls such people to guidance, they will never accept it. Once hearts are sealed due to persistent rejection, guidance becomes impossible. This is both a comfort to the Prophet (that their disbelief is not his failure) and a warning to everyone not to let their hearts reach this state.",
  "58": "Despite all the wrongdoing described in previous verses, Allah introduces Himself as forgiving and merciful. His primary attributes are mercy and forgiveness. This is an invitation—even those who have wronged themselves can return to Allah and find forgiveness if they sincerely repent.\n\nIf Allah punished people immediately for every sin, no one would survive. But Allah gives time—time to realize mistakes, time to repent, time to change. This delay is not weakness or indifference; it is mercy. The fact that sinners continue to live and receive blessings is a sign of Allah's patience.\n\nHowever, this patience has a limit. Everyone has an appointed time for accountability—either at death or the Day of Judgment. When that time comes, there will be no escape, no hiding place, no postponement. The wise person uses the time of mercy to prepare for that inevitable appointment.",
  "59": "Allah references the many nations throughout history that He destroyed because of their continuous wrongdoing and rejection of messengers. These include the people of Noah, 'Aad, Thamud, Lot, and others mentioned throughout the Quran. Their ruins still exist as warnings for those who reflect.\n\nEach of these nations was given time before their destruction—time during which messengers warned them and called them to repentance. But when they persisted in rejection and the appointed time arrived, destruction came. This pattern shows that Allah's patience is not unlimited and that consequences eventually come for persistent wrongdoing.",
  "60": "Allah begins the story of Moses's journey to seek knowledge. His companion was Yusha' ibn Nun (Joshua), who would later become a prophet himself. He was young and served as Moses's assistant, showing that seeking knowledge often involves having good companionship.\n\nMoses declared his firm determination to reach a specific location where two bodies of water meet. According to scholars, this is where he was told he would find a man with special knowledge. Moses was already one of the greatest prophets, yet he was willing to undertake a long journey to learn more. This teaches us that seeking knowledge requires determination and effort.\n\nMoses was willing to travel for an extremely long time—years if necessary—to reach his destination. This shows his extraordinary commitment to learning. No journey is too long, no effort too great, when seeking beneficial knowledge. This should inspire us to make sacrifices in pursuit of knowledge.",
  "61": "When they arrived at their destination, they somehow forgot about the fish they had brought as food. Moses had been told that the sign of finding the special person would involve the fish. The 'forgetting' was part of Allah's plan to create the sign.\n\nMiraculously, the fish—which had been salted and prepared for eating—came back to life and swam into the sea. It left a tunnel-like path in the water as it escaped. This was the miraculous sign that Moses had been waiting for, but in their tiredness, they didn't notice it happening.",
  "62": "After traveling past the meeting point, Moses felt hungry and asked Joshua for the fish they had brought. This was a natural request after a long, tiring journey.\n\nMoses acknowledged that this part of the journey had been particularly tiring. Interestingly, according to the Prophet's explanation, they had not felt fatigue on the journey until they passed the spot where they were supposed to stop. The exhaustion was a sign that they had gone too far.",
  "63": "Joshua recalled the place where they had stopped to rest by a rock. That was the location where the significant event had occurred.\n\nJoshua admitted that he had forgotten the fish at that spot. He took responsibility for his forgetfulness, which shows his honesty and good character.\n\nJoshua attributed his forgetfulness to Satan's influence. This teaches us that even minor forgetfulness can be from Satan's whispers, and we should seek Allah's protection. It also shows that blaming Satan doesn't mean avoiding responsibility—Joshua still acknowledged his role in forgetting.\n\nJoshua described the amazing event: the dead, salted fish came back to life and swam away in an astonishing manner. This was the sign they had been looking for but had missed.",
  "64": "Instead of being upset about the forgotten fish or the extra travel, Moses immediately recognized that this was the sign he had been told about. His focus was on the goal, not on the inconvenience. This shows wisdom and proper prioritization.\n\nThey turned back and followed their own tracks to return to the exact spot where the fish had escaped. Despite their tiredness, they didn't complain but immediately acted on the new information. The seeker of knowledge must be ready to make extra effort when needed.",
  "65": "At the location where the fish had escaped, they found the person they were looking for. This was Al-Khidr, as identified by the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). He is described simply as 'a servant of Ours'—a title of honor that emphasizes his devotion to Allah.\n\nAllah had blessed Al-Khidr with special mercy. This mercy could include prophethood according to some scholars, or a special rank of sainthood according to others. Either way, it was a direct favor from Allah that elevated his status.\n\nAl-Khidr was given special knowledge directly from Allah—knowledge that is not accessible through normal means of learning. This was knowledge of the unseen and the divine wisdom behind events that appear confusing to others. This special knowledge is what Moses came to learn about.",
  "66": "Despite being a great prophet and having spoken directly with Allah, Moses approached Al-Khidr with complete humility. He asked permission to follow him, acknowledging that Al-Khidr had knowledge that he did not. Moses wanted to learn 'right guidance'—wisdom about how things truly work. This teaches us that no matter how much we know, there is always more to learn, and we should approach teachers with humility and respect.",
  "67": "Al-Khidr was honest from the beginning: he told Moses directly that Moses would not be able to remain patient with him. This was not rudeness but truthful assessment. Al-Khidr knew that his actions would appear strange and troubling to anyone who didn't understand the divine wisdom behind them. He was warning Moses about the challenge ahead.",
  "68": "Al-Khidr explained why patience would be difficult: Moses would witness events whose true meaning was hidden from him. When we see something that appears wrong but don't understand the full picture, patience is extremely hard. This principle applies to our lives too—we often question why things happen because we cannot see the complete divine wisdom behind events.",
  "69": "Moses committed to being patient, but wisely added 'Allah willing' (in shaa Allah). He had learned from the earlier teaching in this surah about not making definite promises about the future without acknowledging Allah's will. His promise was conditional on Allah's help.\n\nMoses also promised complete obedience during this learning journey. He understood that as a student, he needed to follow his teacher's guidance without resistance. This shows Moses's genuine commitment to learning, even though he himself was a prophet and leader.",
  "70": "Al-Khidr set a clear condition: Moses must not ask questions about anything until Al-Khidr himself chooses to explain. This was crucial because Al-Khidr would do things that appeared wrong, and questioning would interrupt the lesson. The explanations would come at the right time. This teaches students to trust their teachers and wait for explanations rather than immediately challenging what they don't understand.",
  "71": "They began their journey together and boarded a ship. The ship's owners, recognizing Al-Khidr as a righteous man, allowed them to ride for free. But then Al-Khidr did something shocking: he made a hole in the ship—an act of apparent sabotage that could endanger everyone on board.\n\nMoses immediately reacted. He couldn't stay silent when he saw what appeared to be an act that would harm innocent people. His sense of justice compelled him to speak up. To Moses, this looked like an attempted murder of the passengers.\n\nMoses declared that this was a terrible, monstrous act. From his perspective, Al-Khidr had just endangered the lives of people who had kindly given them free passage. This appeared to be both dangerous and ungrateful behavior.",
  "72": "Al-Khidr reminded Moses of his earlier warning. He had predicted that Moses would not be able to remain patient, and the prediction came true at the very first test. Al-Khidr wasn't angry, just confirming what he had said. This shows how difficult it is to be patient with things we don't understand.",
  "73": "Moses apologized sincerely. He admitted that he had forgotten their agreement in the heat of the moment. He asked for another chance and requested that Al-Khidr not make the journey difficult for him. Moses's humility in admitting his mistake and asking for leniency shows his noble character. A true seeker of knowledge accepts correction gracefully.",
  "74": "They continued their journey and encountered a young boy. Without any apparent provocation, Al-Khidr killed the boy. This was even more shocking than the ship incident—taking an innocent life with no visible reason. To any observer, this would be murder of an innocent child.\n\nMoses was horrified. The boy had not committed any crime deserving death. Moses described him as an innocent soul who had not killed anyone—so there was no justification for taking his life according to any law Moses knew.\n\nMoses declared this to be a horrible, unacceptable act—even worse than the ship incident. While the ship could be repaired, a life cannot be restored. Moses's reaction was based on his understanding of justice, but he was again judging without knowing the full picture.",
  "75": "Al-Khidr again reminded Moses of his warning, this time adding 'tell you' to emphasize that he had specifically warned Moses about this. It was now the second violation of their agreement. Al-Khidr remained calm, understanding that what he did appeared terrible to anyone without knowledge of the divine wisdom behind it.",
  "76": "Moses gave himself an ultimatum: one more objection and Al-Khidr should part ways with him. Moses was essentially saying that if he failed a third time, he would have no more excuses. This shows Moses's determination to control himself and honor his commitment.\n\nMoses acknowledged that three strikes would be enough reason for Al-Khidr to end their companionship. He accepted responsibility in advance for any future failure. This is a fair and honest approach—setting clear consequences for himself.",
  "77": "They continued their journey and arrived at a town. This would be the setting for the third and final incident.\n\nWhen they asked the townspeople for food, they were refused. In Arab culture, hospitality is sacred, and refusing to feed travelers is considered extremely rude and mean. These were inhospitable, stingy people who violated basic standards of decency.\n\nDespite the town's rudeness, Al-Khidr found a wall that was about to fall down and repaired it. This required effort and skill, yet he did this work for free for people who had just refused them basic hospitality.\n\nThis time, Moses's objection was different—not about morality but about practicality and fairness. Why do free work for people who refused to even give them food? At minimum, Al-Khidr could have asked for payment, which would have bought them the food they needed. This seemed like an illogical use of time and energy for ungrateful people.",
  "78": "Al-Khidr announced that their companionship had come to an end. Moses had questioned him three times, breaking their agreement. Though this third objection was milder than the previous ones, it was still a violation of the condition Al-Khidr had set.\n\nBefore parting, Al-Khidr promised to explain the divine wisdom behind each of the three incidents. This was the moment Moses had been waiting for—the explanations would reveal that what appeared to be wrong was actually right when seen from a higher perspective. The lessons from these explanations would benefit not just Moses but all who hear this story until the Day of Judgment.",
  "79": "Al-Khidr begins explaining the first incident. The ship was not owned by wealthy merchants but by poor laborers who depended on it for their livelihood. It was their only means of earning a living. This detail, unknown to Moses at the time, changes everything about understanding what happened.\n\nAl-Khidr deliberately made the ship defective—not to harm the owners but to help them. The damage was intentional and calculated. What appeared to be vandalism was actually an act of protection and mercy.\n\nHere is the hidden wisdom: ahead of them was an oppressive king who was confiscating every functional ship he found. By making the ship appear damaged and defective, Al-Khidr ensured the tyrant would pass it by. A small, repairable hole saved the poor owners from losing their entire ship and livelihood. This teaches us that sometimes what appears to be a loss or harm is actually Allah's protection from something much worse. A minor problem can be a blessing in disguise.",
  "80": "Al-Khidr now explains the most troubling incident. The boy's parents were sincere believers in Allah—righteous people whose faith was genuine. They loved their son dearly, as all parents do.\n\nThrough divine knowledge, it was known that if this boy grew up, he would become such a source of trouble for his parents that he would lead them away from their faith. He would burden them with his rebellion and potentially cause them to commit disbelief—either through his influence or through the grief and trials he would bring upon them. The word 'we' here may indicate Al-Khidr acting on divine command, showing this was Allah's decision, not personal judgment. Sometimes Allah takes something precious from us to protect us from greater harm we cannot foresee.",
  "81": "The taking of this child was coupled with hope for something better. Allah would replace this child with one who would be more pure in character, more righteous, and more loving and dutiful toward the parents. According to some narrations, Allah blessed them with a righteous daughter who later became the mother of a prophet. This teaches us that when Allah takes something from us, He often replaces it with something better—though we may not see it immediately. The Prophet (peace be upon him) taught that when a believer loses a child and is patient, Allah grants them Paradise. Our limited vision cannot see what Allah, in His infinite wisdom, knows is best for us.",
  "82": "Al-Khidr explains the final incident. The wall belonged to two orphaned children who lived in that unwelcoming town. They were vulnerable—without a father to protect their interests.\n\nBeneath that crumbling wall was a buried treasure that rightfully belonged to these orphans. If the wall had collapsed, the treasure would have been exposed and likely stolen by the inhospitable townspeople who had refused even basic hospitality to travelers.\n\nTheir deceased father was a righteous, God-fearing man. Because of his righteousness, Allah protected his children's inheritance even after his death. This is a profound lesson: a parent's righteousness benefits their children. The scholars note that a person's good deeds can protect their family even after they pass away. The Prophet (peace be upon him) encouraged leaving righteous children and ongoing charity as ways to continue earning reward after death.\n\nAllah's plan was for the orphans to grow up and then uncover their treasure themselves when they could properly protect and use it. Repairing the wall ensured it would stand until they matured. This was pure mercy from Allah for these vulnerable children.\n\nAl-Khidr makes a crucial clarification: he did not act based on personal judgment or whim. Everything he did was by Allah's command and divine instruction. He was merely an instrument carrying out Allah's will. This removes any idea that a human can take such actions based on personal reasoning.\n\nAl-Khidr concludes by affirming that these were the explanations Moses could not wait for. Now that the wisdom is revealed, everything makes sense. The lesson for all of us is that Allah's wisdom is behind everything that happens, even when we cannot understand it. Our job is to trust Allah and be patient, knowing that He sees what we cannot see.",
  "83": "Some people asked the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) about Dhul-Qarnain—a famous figure whose story was known among the People of the Book. The name means 'the one with two horns' or 'the one of two ages/eras.' His exact identity is debated by scholars—some say he was a righteous king, others suggest he may have been a prophet. What matters is the lessons from his story.\n\nAllah instructed the Prophet to share part of Dhul-Qarnain's story. The word 'something' indicates that this is not his complete biography but highlights that teach important lessons. Allah selects from history what benefits us spiritually and morally.",
  "84": "Allah granted Dhul-Qarnain power and authority over vast territories. He was not just a local ruler but had dominion over much of the known world. This establishment was from Allah—a reminder that all authority ultimately comes from Him.\n\nAllah provided him with all the resources, knowledge, and means he needed to accomplish his goals. Whether it was military strength, administrative ability, knowledge of languages, or material resources—Allah gave him access to everything necessary. This shows that when Allah wants to empower someone for a mission, He provides all the tools needed.",
  "85": "Dhul-Qarnain used the means Allah gave him and embarked on a journey. Despite having great power, he was not idle or self-indulgent. He actively traveled to fulfill his purpose. This shows that having resources comes with responsibility to use them properly.",
  "86": "He traveled westward until he reached the westernmost point accessible to him—where the sun appeared to set from his perspective. This does not mean he reached where the sun literally sets, but rather the farthest western land he could reach.\n\nFrom his vantage point, the sun appeared to set into a body of dark, muddy water—perhaps an ocean or large sea at sunset. This is describing his visual perception, not making a scientific claim about where the sun actually goes. Anyone watching sunset over an ocean sees the sun appear to sink into the water.\n\nAt this western frontier, he encountered a nation of people. As a powerful ruler, he now had to decide how to deal with them.\n\nAllah gave Dhul-Qarnain authority and discretion in dealing with these people. He could punish them if they deserved it, or he could treat them with kindness. This was a test of his wisdom, justice, and character. Power reveals character—how would this mighty ruler use his authority?",
  "87": "Dhul-Qarnain established a just system: wrongdoers would face consequences. He would not let oppression and evil go unchecked. This shows that a righteous ruler maintains justice and does not tolerate wrongdoing.\n\nHe understood that his punishment was only for this world; ultimate judgment belongs to Allah. Wrongdoers face consequences in this life and then face Allah for even greater accountability. Dhul-Qarnain saw his role as implementing justice while recognizing Allah's supreme authority over final judgment.",
  "88": "Dhul-Qarnain equally promised reward for righteousness. Believers who do good deeds would receive excellent treatment and reward. A just ruler punishes wrongdoing but also recognizes and rewards virtue.\n\nHe would give righteous people light responsibilities and treat them with gentleness. This shows his balanced approach—strict with criminals but gentle with good people. This is the model of just governance: firm against evil, kind toward good.",
  "89": "After completing his mission in the west, Dhul-Qarnain did not rest. He embarked on another journey, this time heading in a different direction—eastward. His commitment to his mission was continuous and tireless.",
  "90": "He traveled eastward until he reached the farthest eastern lands accessible to him—where the sun appeared to rise from his perspective.\n\nHe discovered a people living in primitive conditions with no buildings, trees, or caves to shelter them from the sun. They were completely exposed to the elements. Some scholars suggest these people may have lived underground or in tunnels during the day to escape the heat. This shows the diversity of human conditions that Dhul-Qarnain encountered in his travels.",
  "91": "Allah confirms that He was fully aware of Dhul-Qarnain's situation, his actions, and everything about him. Nothing escaped Allah's knowledge. This is both an affirmation of Dhul-Qarnain's righteousness (Allah knew and approved of his conduct) and a reminder that Allah knows everything about everyone.",
  "92": "Dhul-Qarnain embarked on yet another journey—his third expedition. His energy and dedication to his mission were remarkable. He continued to use the resources Allah gave him to benefit humanity.",
  "93": "This time his journey brought him to a location between two great mountains—a strategic pass or valley.\n\nThe people living there spoke a language so different that communication was extremely difficult. Despite his resources and abilities, there was a significant language barrier. This did not stop him from trying to help them—he found ways to communicate.",
  "94": "Despite the language barrier, these people managed to convey their desperate situation. They were being terrorized by two groups called Gog and Magog (Ya'juj and Ma'juj)—tribes known for causing widespread destruction and corruption. These people were suffering greatly from their raids and attacks.\n\nThey offered to pay Dhul-Qarnain tribute or taxes if he would build a barrier to protect them from Gog and Magog. They were willing to contribute financially because they recognized their inability to solve this problem alone. This shows they trusted this righteous ruler to help them.",
  "95": "Dhul-Qarnain declined their payment. What Allah had given him was more than enough—he had no need for their money. This shows his lack of greed and his understanding that his wealth was from Allah, not a payment for services. He was not motivated by money but by righteousness.\n\nInstead of payment, he asked for their labor and material assistance. He wanted them to participate in their own protection rather than just paying someone else to do it. This approach builds community involvement and ensures the people value and maintain what is built. He promised to construct not just a wall but a strong barrier (radm) that would effectively block Gog and Magog.",
  "96": "Dhul-Qarnain began directing the construction project by requesting large pieces of iron. He used the strongest available materials, showing his engineering knowledge and practical wisdom.\n\nThe iron blocks were stacked until they filled the entire pass between the two mountains. The barrier reached from one mountain to the other, blocking the route completely.\n\nHe commanded workers to use bellows to fan flames and heat the iron. This was an industrial-scale metalworking operation requiring coordinated effort from many people.\n\nOnce the iron was heated to a red-hot state, he had molten copper poured over it. The copper would fill gaps between the iron blocks and, when cooled, create an incredibly strong, solid structure. This combination of iron and copper created a barrier that could not be easily broken through, climbed over, or tunneled under. The engineering sophistication is remarkable.",
  "97": "The barrier was completely effective. Gog and Magog could not climb over it—it was too high, too smooth, or both. They could not dig through it either—the iron and copper construction was impenetrable with their tools. The people were finally safe from the corruption and violence they had suffered.",
  "98": "Despite his incredible achievement, Dhul-Qarnain did not boast or take personal credit. He immediately attributed it to Allah's mercy. The ability to build it, the resources used, the success of the project—all were from Allah. This humility after great accomplishment is the mark of true righteousness.\n\nDhul-Qarnain acknowledged that this barrier, as strong as it was, would not last forever. When Allah's appointed time comes—near the Day of Judgment—this barrier will be destroyed and Gog and Magog will emerge. Nothing human-made is permanent; only Allah's decree is eternal.\n\nHe affirmed complete faith in Allah's promises. Whatever Allah decrees will certainly happen. This is a statement of pure belief that everything—construction and destruction—happens according to Allah's plan.",
  "99": "When the barrier is finally broken near the end of times, Gog and Magog will pour out like waves, crashing into each other and spreading across the earth. Their emergence is one of the major signs of the Day of Judgment mentioned in authentic hadiths. The chaos will be immense as these tribes spread corruption everywhere.\n\nAfter the emergence of Gog and Magog and other major signs, the Trumpet will be blown, signaling the end of this world and the beginning of the Day of Judgment. All of humanity—from the first person to the last, including Gog and Magog—will be gathered before Allah for final judgment. No one will escape this gathering. The story of Dhul-Qarnain thus connects to the ultimate reality: no matter how mighty our works, everything ends with standing before Allah.",
  "100": "On the Day of Judgment, Hell will be brought forth and shown openly to those who disbelieved. It will not be hidden or distant—they will see it clearly before them. This display serves as the fulfillment of the warnings they rejected during their worldly lives. What they denied and mocked will now be undeniably visible. The Prophet (peace be upon him) described that Hell will be brought with seventy thousand reins, each held by seventy thousand angels. The sight will be terrifying beyond imagination.",
  "101": "These disbelievers are described as people whose eyes were covered when it came to Allah's reminders—the Quran, the signs in creation, and the messages of the prophets. They had physical eyes that could see worldly matters clearly, but when it came to spiritual truth, they deliberately looked away. They chose blindness over sight when facing divine guidance.\n\nNot only did they refuse to see, but they also could not bear to hear Allah's message. When the Quran was recited or when they were reminded of Allah, they found it unbearable and turned away. Some would literally cover their ears, while others would mock or create noise to avoid hearing. This wasn't an inability but a stubborn refusal disguised as inability. They hated the truth so much that even hearing it was painful to them.",
  "102": "Allah poses a rhetorical question exposing the foolishness of those who worship created beings instead of the Creator. Some worshipped angels, prophets, righteous people, or idols—all of whom are Allah's servants, not lords. How illogical is it to worship the servant while ignoring the Master? Those being worshipped never asked for worship and will themselves reject their false worshippers on the Day of Judgment.\n\nAllah has already prepared Hell as the lodging place for disbelievers. The word 'accommodation' or 'hospitality' is used ironically—where a guest would normally expect comfort and welcome, the disbelievers will find only punishment and torment. This is the 'welcome' that awaits those who rejected Allah's true welcome into His mercy and guidance.",
  "103": "Allah instructs the Prophet to ask people a thought-provoking question: Do you want to know who the biggest losers are? Not losers in wealth or status, but losers in terms of their deeds—their efforts and life's work. This introduction creates curiosity and prepares the listener for an important lesson that might challenge their assumptions.",
  "104": "The greatest losers are those who worked hard, exerted effort, and were busy throughout their lives—but all of it was wasted. Their striving led nowhere beneficial. This is not about lazy people but about people who were actively working, building, achieving—but in the wrong direction.\n\nThe most tragic part is that these losers genuinely believed they were doing excellent work. They were self-satisfied and confident in their achievements. They saw themselves as successful, progressive, and virtuous. This self-deception is what makes their loss so complete—they never sought to correct their course because they thought they were already on the right path. This verse should make every person reflect: Am I truly on the right path, or am I deceiving myself?",
  "105": "Allah identifies who these losers are: those who denied Allah's signs—whether in scripture or in creation—and denied that they would ever meet Allah for judgment. Disbelief and denial of accountability are the core problems that rendered all their work worthless.\n\nBecause of their disbelief, all their deeds become nullified. Even if they did things that appeared good—charity, kindness, achievements—without faith and correct intention, these deeds have no value with Allah. Good deeds must be built on the foundation of faith to count.\n\nOn the Day of Judgment, when deeds are weighed on the divine scale, the deeds of these people will have no weight at all. They will be as if nothing was done. Imagine working your entire life only to discover that your paycheck is worthless. This is the spiritual bankruptcy of those who lived without faith.",
  "106": "The consequence of their wasted lives is Hell. This is not arbitrary punishment but the natural result of their choices. They chose to reject guidance, so they are separated from Allah's mercy. They chose darkness, so they receive darkness.\n\nHell is the recompense for their fundamental rejection of truth. They were shown the way but refused to take it. They were warned but ignored the warnings. Disbelief, when it is a choice made despite clear evidence, carries the heaviest consequences.\n\nBeyond mere disbelief, they actively mocked Allah's revelations and the prophets who brought them. They ridiculed what was sacred, made jokes about divine warnings, and treated Allah's message with contempt. This mockery added insult to their rejection and made their crime even more severe.",
  "107": "In beautiful contrast to the previous verses about losers, Allah describes the true winners. Those who combined faith with righteous deeds will receive the Gardens of Firdaws—the highest level of Paradise, directly beneath Allah's Throne. The same word 'accommodation' used sarcastically for Hell is used genuinely here—this is their true welcome and hospitality from the Most Generous Host. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'When you ask Allah for Paradise, ask for Firdaws, for it is the highest part of Paradise, in the middle of Paradise, and from it spring the rivers of Paradise, and above it is the Throne of the Most Merciful.'",
  "108": "The believers will remain in Paradise eternally—this is not a temporary vacation but a permanent home. And remarkably, they will never wish to leave or go anywhere else. In this world, even the best experiences eventually bore us, and we seek change. But Paradise is so perfect, so completely satisfying, that its inhabitants will never want to be anywhere else. Every desire is fulfilled, every pleasure is complete, and there is nothing better to seek. This is the ultimate contentment that only Allah can provide.",
  "109": "Allah presents an analogy to help us grasp His infinite knowledge and wisdom. Imagine the entire ocean—all the water in all the seas—converted into ink for writing. This is an unimaginably vast amount of ink.\n\nEven this ocean of ink would be completely exhausted before Allah's words, knowledge, and wisdom could be fully recorded. The ink would run dry while Allah's words remain unfinished. This is because Allah's knowledge is infinite while creation—even something as vast as the ocean—is finite.\n\nEven if you doubled the ocean, adding another ocean's worth of ink, it still would not be enough. You could keep adding oceans infinitely, and still Allah's words would not be exhausted. This powerfully illustrates the absolute infinity of Allah's knowledge compared to the limitations of everything else. A similar verse in Surah Luqman (31:27) mentions that if all the trees were pens and the ocean ink, with seven more oceans added, Allah's words would still not be exhausted. This should inspire awe and humility before our Creator.",
  "110": "The Prophet is commanded to declare his humanity. He is not an angel, not divine, not superhuman—he is a human being like everyone else. He eats, sleeps, feels pain, and will die. This establishes the correct understanding of prophethood: prophets are human beings chosen by Allah to deliver His message, not objects of worship themselves. This corrects the error of those who elevated prophets to divine status.\n\nWhat distinguishes the Prophet is that he receives revelation from Allah. And the core message of that revelation is simple and clear: there is only One God worthy of worship. This is the central message of all prophets throughout history—pure monotheism. No partners, no intermediaries, no divisions in divinity—just One Allah.\n\nThis addresses everyone who desires a good outcome when they meet Allah—whether at death or on Judgment Day. If you want that meeting to be joyful rather than terrifying, if you hope for Allah's pleasure and Paradise, then you must act accordingly.\n\nThe first requirement is righteous action. Faith must be accompanied by good deeds—prayers, charity, kindness, honesty, and all forms of righteousness. Mere claims of faith without action are insufficient. Your deeds must reflect your beliefs. The scholars explain that 'good deeds' means deeds that are correct according to Islamic guidance, not just whatever seems good to the individual.\n\nThe second requirement is pure monotheism—worshipping Allah alone without any partners. This means sincerity in worship, directing all acts of devotion exclusively to Allah. Even good deeds become worthless if they are done for show, for others' approval, or while associating partners with Allah. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that Allah says: 'I am the most self-sufficient and have no need of partners. Whoever does a deed while associating others with Me, I will leave him and his shirk (association).' This final verse beautifully summarizes the entire surah and indeed the entire message of Islam: Believe in One God, do righteous deeds sincerely for Him, and look forward with hope to meeting Him."
};
